---\ntitle: \"Microservices Patterns and Practices\"\ndate: 2024-01-16\ndraft: false\nseries: \"systems-architecture\"\ntags: [\"microservices\", \"distributed-systems\", \"patterns\", \"service-mesh\"]\ncategories: [\"technology\"]\nsummary: \"Deep dive into microservices architecture patterns, service decomposition strategies, and implementation best practices.\"\nseries_order: 2\n---\n\n## Microservices Patterns and Practices\n\nBuilding on our [systems architecture foundation](../modern-systems-architecture/), this article explores the practical implementation of microservices architecture, focusing on proven patterns and real-world considerations.\n\n{{< series-nav \n  series=\"systems-architecture\" \n  previous=\"modern-systems-architecture\"\n  current=\"microservices-patterns-and-practices\" \n  next=\"event-driven-architecture-deep-dive\" \n>}}\n\n{{< callout type=\"info\" title=\"Prerequisites\" >}}\nThis article assumes familiarity with distributed systems concepts. Review the [previous article](../modern-systems-architecture/) for foundational concepts.\n{{< /callout >}}\n\n## Service Decomposition Strategies\n\n### Domain-Driven Design Approach\n\n```python\nimport abc\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Optional\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom enum import Enum\n\nclass BoundedContext(abc.ABC):\n    \"\"\"Abstract base for bounded contexts in DDD\"\"\"\n    \n    def __init__(self, name: str):\n        self.name = name\n        self.aggregates = []\n        self.services = []\n    \n    @abc.abstractmethod\n    def get_ubiquitous_language(self) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        \"\"\"Define domain-specific terminology\"\"\"\n        pass\n    \n    @abc.abstractmethod\n    def identify_aggregates(self) -> List['Aggregate']:\n        \"\"\"Identify aggregate roots within this context\"\"\"\n        pass\n\nclass ServiceBoundary(Enum):\n    STRONG = \"strong\"      # Clear separation, different databases\n    MEDIUM = \"medium\"      # Some shared data, coordinated deployment\n    WEAK = \"weak\"          # Shared database, tight coupling\n\n@dataclass\nclass ServiceDecompositionMetrics:\n    cohesion_score: float          # How related are service responsibilities\n    coupling_score: float          # How dependent is service on others\n    data_consistency_complexity: float\n    team_cognitive_load: float\n    deployment_independence: float\n    \n    def calculate_decomposition_score(self) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Calculate overall decomposition quality score\"\"\"\n        # Higher cohesion and independence = better score\n        # Lower coupling and complexity = better score\n        positive_factors = (self.cohesion_score + self.deployment_independence)\n        negative_factors = (self.coupling_score + self.data_consistency_complexity + \n                          self.team_cognitive_load)\n        \n        return (positive_factors * 2) - negative_factors\n\nclass ServiceDecompositionAnalyzer:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.decomposition_patterns = {\n            'by_business_capability': {\n                'description': 'Align services with business functions',\n                'pros': ['High cohesion', 'Business alignment', 'Team ownership'],\n                'cons': ['May require data synchronization', 'Cross-cutting concerns'],\n                'best_for': 'Stable business domains'\n            },\n            'by_data_model': {\n                'description': 'Each service owns its data model',\n                'pros': ['Data ownership', 'Independent scaling', 'Technology diversity'],\n                'cons': ['Data duplication', 'Eventual consistency challenges'],\n                'best_for': 'Data-intensive applications'\n            },\n            'by_team_structure': {\n                'description': 'Conway\\'s Law - services mirror team structure',\n                'pros': ['Clear ownership', 'Reduced coordination', 'Faster development'],\n                'cons': ['May not align with domain', 'Organizational coupling'],\n                'best_for': 'Large organizations with established teams'\n            },\n            'by_scalability_requirements': {\n                'description': 'Separate services based on performance needs',\n                'pros': ['Optimized resource usage', 'Independent scaling'],\n                'cons': ['May break domain boundaries', 'Increased complexity'],\n                'best_for': 'Performance-critical systems'\n            }\n        }\n    \n    def analyze_decomposition_options(self, domain_model: Dict) -> Dict[str, ServiceDecompositionMetrics]:\n        \"\"\"Analyze different decomposition strategies for a domain\"\"\"\n        \n        # Simulate analysis - in practice would use actual domain analysis\n        analysis_results = {}\n        \n        for pattern_name, pattern_info in self.decomposition_patterns.items():\n            # Calculate metrics based on pattern characteristics\n            metrics = self.calculate_pattern_metrics(pattern_name, domain_model)\n            analysis_results[pattern_name] = metrics\n        \n        return analysis_results\n    \n    def calculate_pattern_metrics(self, pattern_name: str, domain_model: Dict) -> ServiceDecompositionMetrics:\n        \"\"\"Calculate metrics for a specific decomposition pattern\"\"\"\n        \n        # Simplified scoring based on pattern characteristics\n        pattern_scores = {\n            'by_business_capability': {\n                'cohesion_score': 0.9,\n                'coupling_score': 0.3,\n                'data_consistency_complexity': 0.6,\n                'team_cognitive_load': 0.4,\n                'deployment_independence': 0.8\n            },\n            'by_data_model': {\n                'cohesion_score': 0.7,\n                'coupling_score': 0.4,\n                'data_consistency_complexity': 0.8,\n                'team_cognitive_load': 0.5,\n                'deployment_independence': 0.9\n            },\n            'by_team_structure': {\n                'cohesion_score': 0.6,\n                'coupling_score': 0.2,\n                'data_consistency_complexity': 0.5,\n                'team_cognitive_load': 0.3,\n                'deployment_independence': 0.7\n            },\n            'by_scalability_requirements': {\n                'cohesion_score': 0.5,\n                'coupling_score': 0.6,\n                'data_consistency_complexity': 0.7,\n                'team_cognitive_load': 0.6,\n                'deployment_independence': 0.8\n            }\n        }\n        \n        scores = pattern_scores.get(pattern_name, {})\n        return ServiceDecompositionMetrics(**scores)\n    \n    def recommend_decomposition_strategy(self, domain_model: Dict, constraints: Dict) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Recommend the best decomposition strategy given constraints\"\"\"\n        \n        analysis = self.analyze_decomposition_options(domain_model)\n        \n        # Apply constraints to filter recommendations\n        filtered_analysis = {}\n        for pattern_name, metrics in analysis.items():\n            score = metrics.calculate_decomposition_score()\n            \n            # Apply constraint penalties\n            if constraints.get('team_size', 'large') == 'small':\n                # Penalize complex patterns for small teams\n                if metrics.team_cognitive_load > 0.5:\n                    score *= 0.7\n            \n            if constraints.get('data_consistency', 'eventual') == 'strong':\n                # Penalize patterns with high consistency complexity\n                if metrics.data_consistency_complexity > 0.6:\n                    score *= 0.6\n            \n            filtered_analysis[pattern_name] = {\n                'score': score,\n                'metrics': metrics,\n                'pattern_info': self.decomposition_patterns[pattern_name]\n            }\n        \n        # Return best scoring pattern\n        best_pattern = max(filtered_analysis.items(), key=lambda x: x[1]['score'])\n        return best_pattern[0], best_pattern[1]\n\n# Example usage\ndomain_model = {\n    'entities': ['User', 'Order', 'Product', 'Inventory', 'Payment'],\n    'relationships': [\n        ('User', 'Order', 'one-to-many'),\n        ('Order', 'Product', 'many-to-many'),\n        ('Product', 'Inventory', 'one-to-one'),\n        ('Order', 'Payment', 'one-to-one')\n    ],\n    'business_capabilities': [\n        'User Management', 'Order Processing', 'Inventory Management', 'Payment Processing'\n    ]\n}\n\nconstraints = {\n    'team_size': 'medium',\n    'data_consistency': 'eventual',\n    'performance_requirements': 'high',\n    'organizational_maturity': 'medium'\n}\n\nanalyzer = ServiceDecompositionAnalyzer()\nrecommended_pattern, analysis = analyzer.recommend_decomposition_strategy(domain_model, constraints)\n\nprint(f\"Recommended decomposition pattern: {recommended_pattern}\")\nprint(f\"Score: {analysis['score']:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Description: {analysis['pattern_info']['description']}\")\nprint(f\"Best for: {analysis['pattern_info']['best_for']}\")\n```\n\n### Service Interface Design Patterns\n\n```python\n# API Gateway pattern implementation\nclass APIGateway:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.routes = {}\n        self.middleware = []\n        self.service_registry = ServiceRegistry()\n        self.circuit_breaker = CircuitBreaker()\n        self.rate_limiter = RateLimiter()\n    \n    def register_route(self, path_pattern: str, service_name: str, method: str = 'GET'):\n        \"\"\"Register a route to a backend service\"\"\"\n        self.routes[f\"{method}:{path_pattern}\"] = service_name\n    \n    async def handle_request(self, request: 'HTTPRequest') -> 'HTTPResponse':\n        \"\"\"Handle incoming request with full gateway functionality\"\"\"\n        \n        # 1. Authentication & Authorization\n        auth_result = await self.authenticate_request(request)\n        if not auth_result.is_valid:\n            return HTTPResponse(status=401, body={'error': 'Unauthorized'})\n        \n        # 2. Rate limiting\n        if not await self.rate_limiter.allow_request(request.client_id):\n            return HTTPResponse(status=429, body={'error': 'Rate limit exceeded'})\n        \n        # 3. Route resolution\n        route_key = f\"{request.method}:{request.path}\"\n        service_name = self.find_matching_service(route_key)\n        \n        if not service_name:\n            return HTTPResponse(status=404, body={'error': 'Route not found'})\n        \n        # 4. Service discovery\n        service_instance = await self.service_registry.discover_service(service_name)\n        \n        # 5. Circuit breaker check\n        if self.circuit_breaker.is_open(service_name):\n            return HTTPResponse(status=503, body={'error': 'Service unavailable'})\n        \n        # 6. Request transformation\n        transformed_request = await self.transform_request(request, service_instance)\n        \n        try:\n            # 7. Forward request to service\n            response = await self.forward_request(transformed_request, service_instance)\n            \n            # 8. Response transformation\n            final_response = await self.transform_response(response, request)\n            \n            # 9. Update circuit breaker with success\n            self.circuit_breaker.record_success(service_name)\n            \n            return final_response\n            \n        except Exception as e:\n            # 10. Handle failures\n            self.circuit_breaker.record_failure(service_name)\n            \n            # Try fallback if available\n            fallback_response = await self.try_fallback(request, service_name, e)\n            if fallback_response:\n                return fallback_response\n            \n            return HTTPResponse(status=500, body={'error': 'Internal server error'})\n    \n    def find_matching_service(self, route_key: str) -> Optional[str]:\n        \"\"\"Find service for route with pattern matching\"\"\"\n        # Simple exact match - in practice would use regex patterns\n        return self.routes.get(route_key)\n    \n    async def transform_request(self, request: 'HTTPRequest', service: 'ServiceInstance') -> 'HTTPRequest':\n        \"\"\"Transform request for backend service\"\"\"\n        # Add correlation ID\n        request.headers['X-Correlation-ID'] = generate_correlation_id()\n        \n        # Add service-specific headers\n        request.headers['X-Service-Version'] = service.version\n        \n        # Transform path if needed\n        request.path = request.path.replace('/api/v1', '')\n        \n        return request\n    \n    async def transform_response(self, response: 'HTTPResponse', original_request: 'HTTPRequest') -> 'HTTPResponse':\n        \"\"\"Transform response for client\"\"\"\n        # Add API version header\n        response.headers['X-API-Version'] = 'v1'\n        \n        # Add CORS headers if needed\n        if original_request.headers.get('Origin'):\n            response.headers['Access-Control-Allow-Origin'] = '*'\n        \n        return response\n\nclass ServiceRegistry:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.services = {}\n        self.health_checker = HealthChecker()\n    \n    def register_service(self, name: str, instance: 'ServiceInstance'):\n        \"\"\"Register a service instance\"\"\"\n        if name not in self.services:\n            self.services[name] = []\n        self.services[name].append(instance)\n    \n    async def discover_service(self, name: str) -> Optional['ServiceInstance']:\n        \"\"\"Discover healthy service instance using load balancing\"\"\"\n        instances = self.services.get(name, [])\n        \n        # Filter healthy instances\n        healthy_instances = []\n        for instance in instances:\n            if await self.health_checker.is_healthy(instance):\n                healthy_instances.append(instance)\n        \n        if not healthy_instances:\n            return None\n        \n        # Simple round-robin load balancing\n        return self.round_robin_select(healthy_instances)\n    \n    def round_robin_select(self, instances: List['ServiceInstance']) -> 'ServiceInstance':\n        \"\"\"Simple round-robin load balancing\"\"\"\n        if not hasattr(self, '_round_robin_counter'):\n            self._round_robin_counter = 0\n        \n        selected = instances[self._round_robin_counter % len(instances)]\n        self._round_robin_counter += 1\n        return selected\n\nclass CircuitBreaker:\n    def __init__(self, failure_threshold: int = 5, timeout: int = 60):\n        self.failure_threshold = failure_threshold\n        self.timeout = timeout\n        self.failure_counts = {}\n        self.last_failure_times = {}\n        self.states = {}  # CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN\n    \n    def is_open(self, service_name: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Check if circuit breaker is open for service\"\"\"\n        state = self.states.get(service_name, 'CLOSED')\n        \n        if state == 'CLOSED':\n            return False\n        elif state == 'OPEN':\n            # Check if timeout has passed\n            last_failure = self.last_failure_times.get(service_name, 0)\n            if time.time() - last_failure > self.timeout:\n                self.states[service_name] = 'HALF_OPEN'\n                return False\n            return True\n        elif state == 'HALF_OPEN':\n            return False\n    \n    def record_success(self, service_name: str):\n        \"\"\"Record successful request\"\"\"\n        self.failure_counts[service_name] = 0\n        if self.states.get(service_name) == 'HALF_OPEN':\n            self.states[service_name] = 'CLOSED'\n    \n    def record_failure(self, service_name: str):\n        \"\"\"Record failed request\"\"\"\n        import time\n        \n        self.failure_counts[service_name] = self.failure_counts.get(service_name, 0) + 1\n        self.last_failure_times[service_name] = time.time()\n        \n        if self.failure_counts[service_name] >= self.failure_threshold:\n            self.states[service_name] = 'OPEN'\n\n# Data classes for type hints\n@dataclass\nclass HTTPRequest:\n    method: str\n    path: str\n    headers: Dict[str, str]\n    body: Optional[str] = None\n    client_id: Optional[str] = None\n\n@dataclass\nclass HTTPResponse:\n    status: int\n    body: Dict\n    headers: Dict[str, str] = None\n    \n    def __post_init__(self):\n        if self.headers is None:\n            self.headers = {}\n\n@dataclass\nclass ServiceInstance:\n    name: str\n    host: str\n    port: int\n    version: str\n    health_check_url: str\n\n@dataclass\nclass AuthResult:\n    is_valid: bool\n    user_id: Optional[str] = None\n    permissions: List[str] = None\n\ndef generate_correlation_id() -> str:\n    import uuid\n    return str(uuid.uuid4())\n```\n\n## Inter-Service Communication Patterns\n\n### Synchronous Communication\n\n{{< code-tabs >}}\n```python\n# HTTP REST with retries and circuit breakers\nimport asyncio\nimport aiohttp\nfrom typing import Optional, Dict, Any\nimport backoff\n\nclass HTTPServiceClient:\n    def __init__(self, base_url: str, timeout: int = 30):\n        self.base_url = base_url\n        self.timeout = aiohttp.ClientTimeout(total=timeout)\n        self.session: Optional[aiohttp.ClientSession] = None\n    \n    async def __aenter__(self):\n        self.session = aiohttp.ClientSession(timeout=self.timeout)\n        return self\n    \n    async def __aexit__(self, exc_type, exc_val, exc_tb):\n        if self.session:\n            await self.session.close()\n    \n    @backoff.on_exception(\n        backoff.expo,\n        (aiohttp.ClientError, asyncio.TimeoutError),\n        max_tries=3,\n        max_time=60\n    )\n    async def get(self, path: str, headers: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"GET request with exponential backoff retry\"\"\"\n        url = f\"{self.base_url}/{path.lstrip('/')}\"\n        \n        async with self.session.get(url, headers=headers) as response:\n            response.raise_for_status()\n            return await response.json()\n    \n    @backoff.on_exception(\n        backoff.expo,\n        (aiohttp.ClientError, asyncio.TimeoutError),\n        max_tries=3,\n        max_time=60\n    )\n    async def post(self, path: str, data: Dict[str, Any], \n                  headers: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"POST request with exponential backoff retry\"\"\"\n        url = f\"{self.base_url}/{path.lstrip('/')}\"\n        \n        async with self.session.post(url, json=data, headers=headers) as response:\n            response.raise_for_status()\n            return await response.json()\n```\n\n```go\n// gRPC implementation in Go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n    \"context\"\n    \"log\"\n    \"time\"\n    \"google.golang.org/grpc\"\n    \"google.golang.org/grpc/credentials/insecure\"\n    \"google.golang.org/grpc/keepalive\"\n)\n\ntype ServiceClient struct {\n    conn   *grpc.ClientConn\n    client UserServiceClient\n}\n\nfunc NewServiceClient(address string) (*ServiceClient, error) {\n    // Configure connection with keepalive and retry policies\n    conn, err := grpc.Dial(address,\n        grpc.WithTransportCredentials(insecure.NewCredentials()),\n        grpc.WithKeepaliveParams(keepalive.ClientParameters{\n            Time:                10 * time.Second,\n            Timeout:             time.Second,\n            PermitWithoutStream: true,\n        }),\n        grpc.WithDefaultServiceConfig(`{\n            \"methodConfig\": [{\n                \"name\": [{\"service\": \"UserService\"}],\n                \"waitForReady\": true,\n                \"retryPolicy\": {\n                    \"MaxAttempts\": 4,\n                    \"InitialBackoff\": \"0.1s\",\n                    \"MaxBackoff\": \"1s\",\n                    \"BackoffMultiplier\": 2.0,\n                    \"RetryableStatusCodes\": [\"UNAVAILABLE\"]\n                }\n            }]\n        }`),\n    )\n    \n    if err != nil {\n        return nil, err\n    }\n    \n    client := NewUserServiceClient(conn)\n    \n    return &ServiceClient{\n        conn:   conn,\n        client: client,\n    }, nil\n}\n\nfunc (s *ServiceClient) GetUser(ctx context.Context, userID string) (*User, error) {\n    // Add timeout to context\n    ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 5*time.Second)\n    defer cancel()\n    \n    // Add metadata for tracing\n    ctx = metadata.AppendToOutgoingContext(ctx, \n        \"correlation-id\", generateCorrelationID(),\n        \"service-name\", \"user-client\",\n    )\n    \n    request := &GetUserRequest{\n        UserId: userID,\n    }\n    \n    return s.client.GetUser(ctx, request)\n}\n\nfunc (s *ServiceClient) Close() error {\n    return s.conn.Close()\n}\n```\n{{< /code-tabs >}}\n\n### Asynchronous Communication\n\n```python\n# Event-driven communication with message queues\nimport asyncio\nimport json\nfrom typing import Callable, Dict, Any\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass MessageBroker(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def publish(self, topic: str, message: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n        pass\n    \n    @abstractmethod\n    async def subscribe(self, topic: str, handler: Callable) -> None:\n        pass\n\nclass RedisMessageBroker(MessageBroker):\n    def __init__(self, redis_url: str):\n        self.redis_url = redis_url\n        self.redis_client = None\n        self.subscribers = {}\n    \n    async def connect(self):\n        import aioredis\n        self.redis_client = aioredis.from_url(self.redis_url)\n    \n    async def publish(self, topic: str, message: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Publish message to Redis pub/sub\"\"\"\n        message_json = json.dumps(message)\n        await self.redis_client.publish(topic, message_json)\n    \n    async def subscribe(self, topic: str, handler: Callable) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Subscribe to topic with message handler\"\"\"\n        if topic not in self.subscribers:\n            self.subscribers[topic] = []\n        self.subscribers[topic].append(handler)\n        \n        # Start listening if this is the first subscription to this topic\n        if len(self.subscribers[topic]) == 1:\n            asyncio.create_task(self._listen_to_topic(topic))\n    \n    async def _listen_to_topic(self, topic: str):\n        \"\"\"Background task to listen for messages on topic\"\"\"\n        pubsub = self.redis_client.pubsub()\n        await pubsub.subscribe(topic)\n        \n        try:\n            async for message in pubsub.listen():\n                if message['type'] == 'message':\n                    try:\n                        data = json.loads(message['data'])\n                        # Call all handlers for this topic\n                        for handler in self.subscribers.get(topic, []):\n                            asyncio.create_task(handler(data))\n                    except Exception as e:\n                        print(f\"Error processing message on {topic}: {e}\")\n        finally:\n            await pubsub.unsubscribe(topic)\n\nclass EventDrivenService:\n    def __init__(self, service_name: str, message_broker: MessageBroker):\n        self.service_name = service_name\n        self.message_broker = message_broker\n        self.event_handlers = {}\n    \n    async def emit_event(self, event_type: str, payload: Dict[str, Any]):\n        \"\"\"Emit an event to the message broker\"\"\"\n        event = {\n            'event_type': event_type,\n            'payload': payload,\n            'timestamp': time.time(),\n            'source_service': self.service_name,\n            'correlation_id': payload.get('correlation_id', self._generate_id())\n        }\n        \n        topic = f\"events.{event_type}\"\n        await self.message_broker.publish(topic, event)\n        \n        # Log event for debugging\n        print(f\"[{self.service_name}] Emitted event: {event_type}\")\n    \n    async def handle_event(self, event_type: str, handler: Callable):\n        \"\"\"Register event handler\"\"\"\n        topic = f\"events.{event_type}\"\n        \n        async def wrapper(event_data):\n            try:\n                await handler(event_data['payload'])\n                print(f\"[{self.service_name}] Handled event: {event_type}\")\n            except Exception as e:\n                print(f\"[{self.service_name}] Error handling {event_type}: {e}\")\n                # Could implement dead letter queue here\n        \n        await self.message_broker.subscribe(topic, wrapper)\n    \n    def _generate_id(self) -> str:\n        import uuid\n        return str(uuid.uuid4())\n\n# Example service implementations\nclass OrderService(EventDrivenService):\n    def __init__(self, message_broker: MessageBroker):\n        super().__init__(\"OrderService\", message_broker)\n        self.orders = {}\n    \n    async def create_order(self, order_data: Dict[str, Any]):\n        \"\"\"Create order and emit event\"\"\"\n        order_id = self._generate_id()\n        order = {\n            'id': order_id,\n            'user_id': order_data['user_id'],\n            'items': order_data['items'],\n            'total_amount': order_data['total_amount'],\n            'status': 'created'\n        }\n        \n        self.orders[order_id] = order\n        \n        # Emit order created event\n        await self.emit_event('order.created', {\n            'order_id': order_id,\n            'user_id': order['user_id'],\n            'total_amount': order['total_amount'],\n            'correlation_id': order_data.get('correlation_id')\n        })\n        \n        return order\n    \n    async def handle_payment_completed(self, payload: Dict[str, Any]):\n        \"\"\"Handle payment completed event\"\"\"\n        order_id = payload['order_id']\n        if order_id in self.orders:\n            self.orders[order_id]['status'] = 'paid'\n            \n            # Emit order confirmed event\n            await self.emit_event('order.confirmed', {\n                'order_id': order_id,\n                'correlation_id': payload.get('correlation_id')\n            })\n\nclass PaymentService(EventDrivenService):\n    def __init__(self, message_broker: MessageBroker):\n        super().__init__(\"PaymentService\", message_broker)\n    \n    async def handle_order_created(self, payload: Dict[str, Any]):\n        \"\"\"Handle order created event by processing payment\"\"\"\n        order_id = payload['order_id']\n        amount = payload['total_amount']\n        \n        # Simulate payment processing\n        await asyncio.sleep(1)\n        payment_successful = True  # Simulate success\n        \n        if payment_successful:\n            await self.emit_event('payment.completed', {\n                'order_id': order_id,\n                'amount': amount,\n                'correlation_id': payload.get('correlation_id')\n            })\n        else:\n            await self.emit_event('payment.failed', {\n                'order_id': order_id,\n                'reason': 'Insufficient funds',\n                'correlation_id': payload.get('correlation_id')\n            })\n\nclass InventoryService(EventDrivenService):\n    def __init__(self, message_broker: MessageBroker):\n        super().__init__(\"InventoryService\", message_broker)\n        self.inventory = {'item_1': 100, 'item_2': 50}  # Simple inventory\n    \n    async def handle_order_confirmed(self, payload: Dict[str, Any]):\n        \"\"\"Handle order confirmed by reserving inventory\"\"\"\n        order_id = payload['order_id']\n        \n        # Simulate inventory reservation\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.5)\n        \n        await self.emit_event('inventory.reserved', {\n            'order_id': order_id,\n            'correlation_id': payload.get('correlation_id')\n        })\n\n# Example system setup\nasync def setup_event_driven_system():\n    # Initialize message broker\n    broker = RedisMessageBroker(\"redis://localhost:6379\")\n    await broker.connect()\n    \n    # Initialize services\n    order_service = OrderService(broker)\n    payment_service = PaymentService(broker)\n    inventory_service = InventoryService(broker)\n    \n    # Setup event handlers\n    await payment_service.handle_event('order.created', payment_service.handle_order_created)\n    await order_service.handle_event('payment.completed', order_service.handle_payment_completed)\n    await inventory_service.handle_event('order.confirmed', inventory_service.handle_order_confirmed)\n    \n    return order_service, payment_service, inventory_service\n\n# Usage example\nasync def simulate_order_flow():\n    order_service, payment_service, inventory_service = await setup_event_driven_system()\n    \n    # Create an order\n    order = await order_service.create_order({\n        'user_id': 'user_123',\n        'items': [{'id': 'item_1', 'quantity': 2}],\n        'total_amount': 29.99,\n        'correlation_id': 'order_flow_001'\n    })\n    \n    print(f\"Created order: {order['id']}\")\n    \n    # Wait for event processing\n    await asyncio.sleep(3)\n    \n    print(f\"Final order status: {order_service.orders[order['id']]['status']}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import time\n    asyncio.run(simulate_order_flow())\n```\n\n## Data Management Patterns\n\n### Saga Pattern for Distributed Transactions\n\n{{< callout type=\"warning\" title=\"Data Consistency\" >}}\nDistributed transactions are complex. Use saga patterns for business processes that span multiple services while maintaining eventual consistency.\n{{< /callout >}}\n\n```python\n# Saga orchestrator pattern\nfrom enum import Enum\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any, Optional\nimport asyncio\n\nclass SagaStep:\n    def __init__(self, name: str, action: Callable, compensation: Callable):\n        self.name = name\n        self.action = action  # Forward action\n        self.compensation = compensation  # Rollback action\n        self.completed = False\n        self.compensated = False\n\nclass SagaState(Enum):\n    PENDING = \"pending\"\n    RUNNING = \"running\"\n    COMPLETED = \"completed\"\n    COMPENSATING = \"compensating\"\n    FAILED = \"failed\"\n    COMPENSATED = \"compensated\"\n\nclass SagaOrchestrator:\n    def __init__(self, saga_id: str, steps: List[SagaStep]):\n        self.saga_id = saga_id\n        self.steps = steps\n        self.state = SagaState.PENDING\n        self.current_step = 0\n        self.context = {}  # Shared data between steps\n        self.execution_log = []\n    \n    async def execute(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Execute saga with compensation on failure\"\"\"\n        self.state = SagaState.RUNNING\n        self.log_event(\"saga_started\", {\"saga_id\": self.saga_id})\n        \n        try:\n            # Execute all steps in sequence\n            for i, step in enumerate(self.steps):\n                self.current_step = i\n                self.log_event(\"step_started\", {\"step_name\": step.name})\n                \n                try:\n                    # Execute forward action\n                    result = await step.action(self.context)\n                    \n                    # Update context with step result\n                    if result:\n                        self.context.update(result)\n                    \n                    step.completed = True\n                    self.log_event(\"step_completed\", {\n                        \"step_name\": step.name,\n                        \"result\": result\n                    })\n                    \n                except Exception as e:\n                    self.log_event(\"step_failed\", {\n                        \"step_name\": step.name,\n                        \"error\": str(e)\n                    })\n                    \n                    # Compensate all completed steps\n                    await self.compensate()\n                    return False\n            \n            # All steps completed successfully\n            self.state = SagaState.COMPLETED\n            self.log_event(\"saga_completed\", {\"saga_id\": self.saga_id})\n            return True\n            \n        except Exception as e:\n            self.log_event(\"saga_error\", {\"error\": str(e)})\n            await self.compensate()\n            return False\n    \n    async def compensate(self):\n        \"\"\"Compensate completed steps in reverse order\"\"\"\n        self.state = SagaState.COMPENSATING\n        self.log_event(\"compensation_started\", {\"saga_id\": self.saga_id})\n        \n        # Compensate in reverse order\n        for i in range(self.current_step, -1, -1):\n            step = self.steps[i]\n            \n            if step.completed and not step.compensated:\n                try:\n                    self.log_event(\"compensation_step_started\", {\n                        \"step_name\": step.name\n                    })\n                    \n                    await step.compensation(self.context)\n                    step.compensated = True\n                    \n                    self.log_event(\"compensation_step_completed\", {\n                        \"step_name\": step.name\n                    })\n                    \n                except Exception as e:\n                    self.log_event(\"compensation_step_failed\", {\n                        \"step_name\": step.name,\n                        \"error\": str(e)\n                    })\n                    # Continue compensating other steps even if one fails\n        \n        self.state = SagaState.COMPENSATED\n        self.log_event(\"compensation_completed\", {\"saga_id\": self.saga_id})\n    \n    def log_event(self, event_type: str, data: Dict[str, Any]):\n        \"\"\"Log saga execution events\"\"\"\n        import time\n        \n        log_entry = {\n            'timestamp': time.time(),\n            'saga_id': self.saga_id,\n            'event_type': event_type,\n            'data': data,\n            'state': self.state.value\n        }\n        \n        self.execution_log.append(log_entry)\n        print(f\"[SAGA-{self.saga_id}] {event_type}: {data}\")\n\n# Example: Order processing saga\nclass OrderProcessingSaga:\n    def __init__(self, order_data: Dict[str, Any]):\n        self.order_data = order_data\n        self.order_service = OrderService()\n        self.payment_service = PaymentService()\n        self.inventory_service = InventoryService()\n        self.shipping_service = ShippingService()\n    \n    def create_saga(self) -> SagaOrchestrator:\n        \"\"\"Create saga with all required steps\"\"\"\n        saga_id = f\"order_saga_{self.order_data['order_id']}\"\n        \n        steps = [\n            SagaStep(\n                name=\"create_order\",\n                action=self.create_order_action,\n                compensation=self.cancel_order_compensation\n            ),\n            SagaStep(\n                name=\"reserve_inventory\",\n                action=self.reserve_inventory_action,\n                compensation=self.release_inventory_compensation\n            ),\n            SagaStep(\n                name=\"process_payment\",\n                action=self.process_payment_action,\n                compensation=self.refund_payment_compensation\n            ),\n            SagaStep(\n                name=\"create_shipment\",\n                action=self.create_shipment_action,\n                compensation=self.cancel_shipment_compensation\n            )\n        ]\n        \n        return SagaOrchestrator(saga_id, steps)\n    \n    # Step actions\n    async def create_order_action(self, context: Dict) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Create order in order service\"\"\"\n        order = await self.order_service.create_order(self.order_data)\n        return {'order': order}\n    \n    async def reserve_inventory_action(self, context: Dict) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Reserve inventory for order items\"\"\"\n        order = context['order']\n        reservation = await self.inventory_service.reserve_items(order['items'])\n        return {'inventory_reservation': reservation}\n    \n    async def process_payment_action(self, context: Dict) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Process payment for order\"\"\"\n        order = context['order']\n        payment = await self.payment_service.charge_payment(\n            order['payment_method'],\n            order['total_amount']\n        )\n        return {'payment': payment}\n    \n    async def create_shipment_action(self, context: Dict) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Create shipment for order\"\"\"\n        order = context['order']\n        shipment = await self.shipping_service.create_shipment({\n            'order_id': order['id'],\n            'address': order['shipping_address'],\n            'items': order['items']\n        })\n        return {'shipment': shipment}\n    \n    # Compensation actions\n    async def cancel_order_compensation(self, context: Dict):\n        \"\"\"Cancel the created order\"\"\"\n        if 'order' in context:\n            await self.order_service.cancel_order(context['order']['id'])\n    \n    async def release_inventory_compensation(self, context: Dict):\n        \"\"\"Release reserved inventory\"\"\"\n        if 'inventory_reservation' in context:\n            await self.inventory_service.release_reservation(\n                context['inventory_reservation']['id']\n            )\n    \n    async def refund_payment_compensation(self, context: Dict):\n        \"\"\"Refund processed payment\"\"\"\n        if 'payment' in context:\n            await self.payment_service.refund_payment(\n                context['payment']['transaction_id']\n            )\n    \n    async def cancel_shipment_compensation(self, context: Dict):\n        \"\"\"Cancel created shipment\"\"\"\n        if 'shipment' in context:\n            await self.shipping_service.cancel_shipment(\n                context['shipment']['id']\n            )\n\n# Usage example\nasync def process_order_with_saga():\n    order_data = {\n        'order_id': 'order_001',\n        'user_id': 'user_123',\n        'items': [{'product_id': 'prod_1', 'quantity': 2}],\n        'total_amount': 99.99,\n        'payment_method': {'type': 'credit_card', 'token': 'card_token'},\n        'shipping_address': {'street': '123 Main St', 'city': 'Anytown'}\n    }\n    \n    # Create and execute saga\n    order_saga = OrderProcessingSaga(order_data)\n    saga_orchestrator = order_saga.create_saga()\n    \n    success = await saga_orchestrator.execute()\n    \n    if success:\n        print(\"Order processed successfully!\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Order processing failed, all changes compensated\")\n    \n    # Print execution log\n    print(\"\\nSaga Execution Log:\")\n    for log_entry in saga_orchestrator.execution_log:\n        print(f\"{log_entry['timestamp']}: {log_entry['event_type']} - {log_entry['data']}\")\n\n# Mock service implementations for the example\nclass OrderService:\n    async def create_order(self, order_data: Dict) -> Dict:\n        print(f\"Creating order: {order_data['order_id']}\")\n        return {'id': order_data['order_id'], **order_data}\n    \n    async def cancel_order(self, order_id: str):\n        print(f\"Cancelling order: {order_id}\")\n\nclass InventoryService:\n    async def reserve_items(self, items: List[Dict]) -> Dict:\n        print(f\"Reserving inventory for {len(items)} items\")\n        return {'id': 'reservation_001', 'items': items}\n    \n    async def release_reservation(self, reservation_id: str):\n        print(f\"Releasing inventory reservation: {reservation_id}\")\n\nclass PaymentService:\n    async def charge_payment(self, payment_method: Dict, amount: float) -> Dict:\n        print(f\"Processing payment of ${amount}\")\n        # Simulate payment failure occasionally\n        if amount > 100:  # Simulate failure for large amounts\n            raise Exception(\"Payment declined - insufficient funds\")\n        return {'transaction_id': 'txn_001', 'amount': amount}\n    \n    async def refund_payment(self, transaction_id: str):\n        print(f\"Refunding payment: {transaction_id}\")\n\nclass ShippingService:\n    async def create_shipment(self, shipment_data: Dict) -> Dict:\n        print(f\"Creating shipment for order: {shipment_data['order_id']}\")\n        return {'id': 'shipment_001', **shipment_data}\n    \n    async def cancel_shipment(self, shipment_id: str):\n        print(f\"Cancelling shipment: {shipment_id}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    asyncio.run(process_order_with_saga())\n```\n\n## Next in This Series\n\nIn our next article, we'll explore **Event-Driven Architecture Deep Dive**, covering:\n\n- Event sourcing patterns\n- CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)\n- Event streaming architectures\n- Distributed event processing\n- Event schema evolution\n\n{{< series-nav \n  series=\"systems-architecture\" \n  previous=\"modern-systems-architecture\"\n  current=\"microservices-patterns-and-practices\" \n  next=\"event-driven-architecture-deep-dive\" \n>}}\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Service decomposition** should align with business domains and team boundaries\n2. **API gateways** provide essential cross-cutting concerns in microservices architectures\n3. **Circuit breakers** and retry policies are crucial for resilient inter-service communication\n4. **Event-driven patterns** enable loose coupling and better scalability\n5. **Saga patterns** handle distributed transactions while maintaining consistency\n\n{{< callout type=\"success\" title=\"Implementation Checklist\" >}}\n✅ **Define service boundaries** using domain-driven design  \n✅ **Implement circuit breakers** for all service communications  \n✅ **Use event-driven patterns** for loose coupling  \n✅ **Plan data consistency** strategy with sagas or event sourcing  \n✅ **Monitor and trace** distributed requests across services  \n{{< /callout >}}\n\n---\n\n*Tags*: microservices, distributed-systems, patterns, service-mesh  \n*Series*: Modern Systems Architecture (2/5)  \n*Category*: Technology > Architecture  \n*Updated*: 2024-01-16